  "worked_solution": "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{\u2032}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System II is decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- Systems I and III are *not* decoupled because their matrices are not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
